==================================================================
DAY 31 - PART A: THEORY
Health Probes: Liveness, Readiness, Startup
Time: 40 minutes
==================================================================

1. "RUNNING" IS NOT "HEALTHY"
-----------------------------
A container can be Running while the app inside is:
  - still starting (loading data for 60 seconds)
  - deadlocked (process alive, never answers)
  - temporarily unable to serve (DB connection lost)
Probes let the kubelet check the app itself.


2. THE THREE PROBES
-------------------
Liveness   "Is it alive?"
           Fails -> kubelet RESTARTS the container.
           For deadlocks/stuck states that only a restart fixes.

Readiness  "Can it serve traffic now?"
           Fails -> pod marked NOT READY and REMOVED from Service
           endpoints. No restart. Checked for the pod's whole life.
           Also gates rolling updates (old pods stay until new ones
           are Ready).

Startup    "Has it finished starting?"
           While it runs, liveness and readiness are paused.
           Fails too long -> restart. For slow-starting apps.


3. PROBE MECHANISMS
-------------------
  httpGet     HTTP GET; 200-399 = success
  tcpSocket   TCP connection opens = success
  exec        command exit code 0 = success
  grpc        gRPC health check protocol


4. TIMING FIELDS
----------------
  initialDelaySeconds  wait before the first probe (default 0)
  periodSeconds        how often (default 10)
  timeoutSeconds       how long to wait for a reply (default 1)
  failureThreshold     consecutive failures to act (default 3)
  successThreshold     consecutive successes to recover (default 1)

Time to restart after a hang ~ periodSeconds x failureThreshold.
Startup budget = failureThreshold x periodSeconds (e.g. 30 x 10 = 300s).


5. EXAMPLE
----------
  containers:
  - name: app
    image: myapp:v1
    startupProbe:
      httpGet: {path: /healthz, port: 8080}
      failureThreshold: 30
      periodSeconds: 5
    livenessProbe:
      httpGet: {path: /healthz, port: 8080}
      periodSeconds: 10
      failureThreshold: 3
    readinessProbe:
      httpGet: {path: /ready, port: 8080}
      periodSeconds: 5


6. GOOD PRACTICES (AND COMMON MISTAKES)
---------------------------------------
- Liveness should check ONLY the process itself. Never check the
  database in liveness: if the DB goes down, every pod restarts in a
  loop and makes things worse. Put dependency checks in READINESS.
- Don't make liveness too aggressive (timeoutSeconds 1 on a busy app
  causes restart storms).
- Use a startup probe instead of a huge initialDelaySeconds.
- Every Service-backed app should have a readiness probe; it's what
  makes rolling updates truly zero-downtime (Day 14 FAILs!).
- Add a preStop sleep (5-10 s) so load balancers stop sending traffic
  before the app shuts down.


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- Liveness fail = restart. Readiness fail = no traffic. Startup =
  protect slow starts.
- Dependencies belong in readiness, not liveness.
- Readiness + preStop = real zero-downtime deploys.
